1
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:51,200
Storm

2
00:02:16,810 --> 00:02:18,550
What the hell?

3
00:02:19,450 --> 00:02:22,310
That can wait until tomorrow.

4
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:24,740
I-It's not my decision.

5
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:28,020
In any case, the mistress insisted.

6
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:29,490
Damn hag.

7
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:33,640
Normally, they leave security up to us.

8
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:37,120
Maybe it's about the three horses
being offered as a reward.

9
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,560
You mean she wants a share?

10
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:41,140
That greedy hag...

11
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,820
Aw, man.

12
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:59,300
Mr. Snake, will you allow me
to see my husband—Gardar?

13
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:00,320
No.

14
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:06,850
He's injured, isn't he? How...

15
00:03:07,610 --> 00:03:09,730
How are his wounds?

16
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:12,660
I don't give a shit.

17
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:16,790
Why should I tend to someone
who killed one of my men?

18
00:03:19,290 --> 00:03:21,270
Boss, your horse.

19
00:03:21,270 --> 00:03:24,420
Right. I'm going to see the mistress.

20
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:28,030
You guys take Arnheid back to Gramps' house.

21
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:39,190
Hey, wait. We don't have any more horses left.

22
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:44,210
Does he seriously expect me to walk you back?

23
00:03:51,520 --> 00:03:54,990
Please let me see my husband.

24
00:03:55,500 --> 00:03:58,520
I swear I'm not planning to help him escape.

25
00:03:58,520 --> 00:04:01,000
I simply want to tend his wounds.

26
00:04:01,550 --> 00:04:03,810
What? But...

27
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:10,090
But the boss said...

28
00:04:20,130 --> 00:04:21,750
Whoa, whoa.

29
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:24,110
What the hell is going on here?

30
00:04:24,440 --> 00:04:27,230
That's Ketil's precious girl.

31
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:33,650
Uh, the boss gave her permission
to treat Gardar's wounds.

32
00:04:33,650 --> 00:04:34,880
It's true.

33
00:04:43,890 --> 00:04:45,010
Gardar...

34
00:04:47,290 --> 00:04:48,630
Arnheid.

35
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:27,040
They beat you.

36
00:05:29,470 --> 00:05:32,710
Cut the ropes, Arnheid.

37
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:35,680
Let's go home.

38
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:37,040
Hey.

39
00:05:37,450 --> 00:05:39,170
It's all right.

40
00:05:39,170 --> 00:05:41,680
She doesn't have anything
to cut the ropes with.

41
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:44,310
That's fine, then.

42
00:05:44,790 --> 00:05:49,690
Hey, Arnheid. Your husband
traveled all the way here to get you.

43
00:05:50,020 --> 00:05:54,070
Why don't you tend to
more than just his wounds?

44
00:05:56,700 --> 00:05:59,750
I can't treat him when he's bound like this.

45
00:06:00,310 --> 00:06:02,200
Could you temporarily untie him?

46
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:07,300
No way. Besides, his junk isn't tied up.

47
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:08,950
You can still take care of that.

48
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:12,390
Yeah.

49
00:06:16,350 --> 00:06:17,780
Arnheid.

50
00:06:22,850 --> 00:06:24,390
I'm sorry.

51
00:06:28,750 --> 00:06:36,130
I'll never leave your side or Hjalti's again.

52
00:06:37,420 --> 00:06:39,150
Never again.

53
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:44,390
I was wrong.

54
00:06:46,330 --> 00:06:50,420
Forgive me, Arnheid.

55
00:07:19,090 --> 00:07:21,220
I put you through so much pain.

56
00:07:22,130 --> 00:07:26,670
Let's start over.
It'll be like it was before.

57
00:07:28,010 --> 00:07:30,480
Release my bonds.

58
00:07:32,870 --> 00:07:38,660
I'm sorry, Gardar. I'm sorry.

59
00:07:44,970 --> 00:07:49,520
What are you talking about?
I should be begging for your forgiveness.

60
00:07:50,930 --> 00:07:55,710
Come on, Arnheid. Pull yourself together.

61
00:08:04,670 --> 00:08:07,040
Well, that spoiled the mood.

62
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:09,780
It's starting to come down hard.

63
00:08:09,780 --> 00:08:11,810
I'm gonna go rest indoors.

64
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:16,420
Hey, we were told to keep watch.

65
00:08:16,810 --> 00:08:18,930
One man's enough.

66
00:08:18,930 --> 00:08:21,180
You're the one who brought her here.

67
00:08:21,530 --> 00:08:23,430
It's your responsibility to watch her.

68
00:08:24,610 --> 00:08:26,180
Seriously?

69
00:08:30,220 --> 00:08:31,070
Hey.

70
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:36,980
Hey, Arnheid. You done over there?

71
00:08:36,980 --> 00:08:38,690
You got what you came for, right?

72
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:51,330
Aw, man. My only flaw is
that I can't say no to women.

73
00:08:52,450 --> 00:08:54,330
Time's up.

74
00:08:56,450 --> 00:08:57,790
Come on, stand up.

75
00:10:10,250 --> 00:10:13,350
Arnheid, there's a knife on that man's hip.

76
00:10:13,350 --> 00:10:15,790
Use that to cut my ropes.

77
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:19,790
Quickly, Arnheid!

78
00:10:22,450 --> 00:10:24,710
Hey, this is fucked! He's dead!

79
00:10:24,710 --> 00:10:29,060
What? He's dead? What the hell is he doing?

80
00:10:26,990 --> 00:10:28,300
Pull yourself together!

81
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:31,430
Quickly, the ropes!

82
00:10:29,510 --> 00:10:30,330
Hurry!

83
00:10:32,750 --> 00:10:34,690
They're going to kill us.

84
00:10:35,540 --> 00:10:37,250
Ready? Let's go.

85
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:42,190
Arnheid!

86
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:09,540
Bad news, boss.

87
00:11:09,540 --> 00:11:11,440
They're all dead.

88
00:11:16,090 --> 00:11:17,840
Those idiots...

89
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:21,600
I told them so many times
not to let their guard down.

90
00:11:26,590 --> 00:11:28,320
Boss, come look at this.

91
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:32,990
It's his sword.

92
00:11:34,210 --> 00:11:36,010
Look at the tip.

93
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:40,470
The rain's washed the blood away,
but there's grease on it.

94
00:11:40,980 --> 00:11:42,740
He stabbed the enemy.

95
00:11:44,330 --> 00:11:45,880
It must've been deep.

96
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:46,710
Yeah.

97
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:49,520
He's got a new injury
on top of his existing one.

98
00:11:50,750 --> 00:11:52,900
There weren't any horses here.

99
00:11:55,050 --> 00:11:56,080
After him!

100
00:11:56,670 --> 00:11:58,750
He can't have made it far!

101
00:11:59,820 --> 00:12:02,600
We're going to settle this
while he's still on the farm!

102
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:05,610
As soon as we find him,

103
00:12:07,490 --> 00:12:08,630
we'll kill him.

104
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:47,300
Couldn't sleep, Einar?

105
00:12:51,410 --> 00:12:53,380
You can't have slept either.

106
00:12:53,950 --> 00:12:56,390
You didn't groan in your sleep,
and you came to wake me.

107
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:00,610
Yeah, I guess.

108
00:13:02,060 --> 00:13:03,920
Were you watching me?

109
00:13:05,140 --> 00:13:09,280
Did you think I'd do something
reckless during the night?

110
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:13,360
I wouldn't do that.

111
00:13:14,120 --> 00:13:16,420
I don't want to cause trouble for Arnheid.

112
00:13:21,450 --> 00:13:23,140
It doesn't make sense.

113
00:13:24,420 --> 00:13:28,030
All we were doing was living quietly.

114
00:13:30,570 --> 00:13:35,380
It's wrong for freedom to be
taken away by violence.

115
00:13:36,410 --> 00:13:37,450
It's wrong.

116
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:53,170
I've spent most of my life at war,
so I have no right to say anything.

117
00:13:54,160 --> 00:14:00,240
But it's wrong. I was wrong.

118
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:08,110
I don't think Arnheid knows
you were a warrior.

119
00:14:10,010 --> 00:14:11,430
It's pathetic.

120
00:14:12,300 --> 00:14:17,330
I'm afraid of someone like her
finding out about my past.

121
00:14:19,020 --> 00:14:23,540
How was I not bothered by
hurting all those people?

122
00:14:26,570 --> 00:14:30,590
I can't even imagine you acting like that.

123
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:38,430
Thorfinn, what you told me the other day...

124
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,710
Were you being serious?

125
00:14:40,710 --> 00:14:41,640
What?

126
00:14:42,560 --> 00:14:48,650
You asked if it was possible to eliminate
war and slavery from the world.

127
00:14:50,050 --> 00:14:52,990
It sounds like a fantastical dream to me,

128
00:14:53,550 --> 00:14:58,660
but as someone who's experienced both,
you must have some ideas.

129
00:15:02,130 --> 00:15:03,310
What should we do?

130
00:15:04,540 --> 00:15:08,720
How can we eliminate war and slavery?

131
00:15:13,530 --> 00:15:17,920
I can only speak about my own experience,

132
00:15:19,950 --> 00:15:23,310
but war always produces lots of slaves.

133
00:15:24,540 --> 00:15:28,870
Those defeated in battle become slaves.

134
00:15:30,080 --> 00:15:33,430
Like you and Arnheid.

135
00:15:35,630 --> 00:15:38,800
The line between warrior
and slave trader is blurry.

136
00:15:40,180 --> 00:15:45,570
Slaves come from other places too,
but I think war is the number one source.

137
00:15:46,280 --> 00:15:49,990
If the number of wars decreased,
so would the number of slaves.

138
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:59,890
But Norse men don't think war is a bad thing.

139
00:16:01,680 --> 00:16:05,720
A Norse man's worth is
determined by his valor and wealth.

140
00:16:06,490 --> 00:16:11,860
The more enemies you kill and wealth you bring
back home, the more you're respected.

141
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:19,440
So fathers teach sons to fight, arm them,
and send them off on warships.

142
00:16:20,430 --> 00:16:25,390
It's what the Nords have done since
ancient times, without questioning it.

143
00:16:27,170 --> 00:16:31,250
It's always difficult to stop doing
what you think is natural.

144
00:16:34,060 --> 00:16:36,750
But there are men like you, too.

145
00:16:37,570 --> 00:16:42,270
Is it really so strange for someone
to swear not to hurt people?

146
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:49,390
In Norse society, cowards can't survive.

147
00:16:54,210 --> 00:16:55,540
But that's fine.

148
00:16:56,480 --> 00:17:01,860
I'd much rather be an outcast
than be burdened by any more of them.

149
00:17:03,890 --> 00:17:05,040
"Them"?

150
00:17:10,720 --> 00:17:12,030
The dead.

151
00:17:14,690 --> 00:17:16,950
The people I killed.

152
00:17:18,860 --> 00:17:24,050
Their ghosts appear every
night to torment me.

153
00:17:24,810 --> 00:17:26,090
"Why did you kill us?"

154
00:17:26,950 --> 00:17:31,750
"You felt so much anger and hate
when your own father was killed,"

155
00:17:32,260 --> 00:17:36,630
"and yet you killed so many
fathers, brothers, and sons."

156
00:17:38,860 --> 00:17:42,930
I need to atone for these people.

157
00:17:43,850 --> 00:17:48,660
I need to take them someplace
they can rest peacefully.

158
00:17:50,200 --> 00:17:53,310
I can't take on any more.

159
00:17:54,210 --> 00:17:57,630
I can't take on a single additional person.

160
00:18:04,890 --> 00:18:10,260
I've been thinking about
what I can do to make it up to them.

161
00:18:11,980 --> 00:18:17,410
You couldn't call simply not killing
or destroying anymore atonement.

162
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:26,150
I need to grow more wheat
than I trampled underfoot.

163
00:18:27,370 --> 00:18:32,080
I need to rebuild more houses
than I burnt down.

164
00:18:33,710 --> 00:18:38,770
I still don't know how to eliminate
war from the entire world.

165
00:18:39,970 --> 00:18:46,530
But just a single village would do.

166
00:18:48,830 --> 00:18:53,150
I want to create a place
where people don't need swords.

167
00:19:04,750 --> 00:19:08,980
But how are you going to defend that land?

168
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:12,450
I don't like war, either.

169
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:16,870
But Vikings eventually show up everywhere.

170
00:19:18,110 --> 00:19:23,030
Sometimes you have to fight
to defend peace and freedom.

171
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:30,660
But that's not good enough.

172
00:19:32,370 --> 00:19:35,120
There's no point if you fight for peace.

173
00:19:35,620 --> 00:19:40,350
You'll never escape from that
bloodthirsty hell that way.

174
00:19:46,770 --> 00:19:53,140
So it'll never be more than just a dream.

175
00:19:55,290 --> 00:20:00,720
You'd have to travel
beyond the ends of the earth.

176
00:20:01,790 --> 00:20:04,500
Somewhere even the Vikings can't reach.

177
00:20:06,090 --> 00:20:11,780
A land for the outcasts.

178
00:20:20,350 --> 00:20:26,570
If I run away to the other side
of the ocean, then what's there?

179
00:20:29,020 --> 00:20:31,860
There's probably nothing there, right?

180
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:36,530
No matter how far I go, it'd be the same as here.

181
00:20:37,440 --> 00:20:40,960
Just filled with wars, and slave traders.

182
00:20:50,590 --> 00:20:54,710
Far to the west, beyond the sea...

183
00:21:01,500 --> 00:21:06,450
Beyond the reach of any authority,
unknown to slave traders,

184
00:21:07,090 --> 00:21:09,940
there is a land far beyond the horizon.

185
00:21:12,950 --> 00:21:15,620
Does a place like that exist?

186
00:21:15,620 --> 00:21:18,550
There is. I'd forgotten.

187
00:21:19,040 --> 00:21:24,120
When I was a child, a sailor
named Leif told me about it.

188
00:21:25,770 --> 00:21:32,770
On the other side of the sea to the west,
there's an untouched, fertile land.

189
00:21:36,160 --> 00:21:38,050
That's amazing.

190
00:21:38,870 --> 00:21:42,190
Then... Then if we go there...

191
00:21:42,190 --> 00:21:43,110
No.

192
00:21:44,540 --> 00:21:49,620
He said it was incredibly far away,
and I don't even know its exact location.

193
00:21:51,810 --> 00:21:53,600
And we'd be establishing a nation.

194
00:21:54,530 --> 00:21:57,510
We'd have to take lots of outcasts with us.

195
00:21:58,320 --> 00:21:59,750
It'd be a huge undertaking.

196
00:22:06,450 --> 00:22:07,590
I see.

197
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:10,920
But...

198
00:22:12,330 --> 00:22:15,970
That's the first bit of good news
I've heard in a while.

199
00:22:48,060 --> 00:22:51,420
Be careful. He's strong.

200
00:22:51,420 --> 00:22:52,430
Got it.

201
00:22:53,880 --> 00:22:57,750
Wh-What's going on? It's still so early.

202
00:23:02,620 --> 00:23:05,790
He doesn't seem to be here.

203
00:23:06,300 --> 00:23:08,790
There's so much crap, it's hard to tell.

204
00:23:09,920 --> 00:23:11,730
Throw it all out.

205
00:23:15,220 --> 00:23:18,000
What the hell are they doing?

206
00:23:20,170 --> 00:23:21,310
He escaped.

207
00:23:22,100 --> 00:23:22,850
What?

208
00:23:23,330 --> 00:23:24,820
Gardar escaped.

209
00:23:25,410 --> 00:23:29,090
What other reason would
they have to search our shed?

210
00:23:35,660 --> 00:23:36,830
Arnheid!

211
00:25:36,960 --> 00:25:39,880
Great Purpose

212
00:25:37,630 --> 00:25:38,750
"Great Purpose."
